# Bodo (Boro) in Devanagari. espeak-ng has no Bodo voice: compatible mode is
# the Hindi reading (as with the reference's Hindi voice); this file is the
# improved reading. Written for uccharan from these descriptions:
#   Pramod Chandra Bhattacharya (1977), A Descriptive Analysis of the Boro
#     Language: the phonemes (six vowels i e a ɔ u ɯ; pʰ tʰ kʰ b d ɡ m n ŋ
#     s z r l h j w; no voiced aspirates, no retroflex or palatal stops), as
#     given by Sarmah (2004) and the Wikipedia article "Boro language (India)";
#   Priyankoo Sarmah (2004), Some Aspects of the Tonal Phonology of Bodo
#     (M.Phil. thesis, CIEFL Hyderabad), and Joseph & Burling (2001): two
#     lexical tones, high and low, one per word on its last syllable;
#   Government of India, "Representation of Bodo, Dogri and Maithili
#     languages in Unicode Standard", Unicode document L2/08-197 (2008): ओ ो
#     (and ऒ ॊ, which the Bodo Sahitya Sabha prefers) write the vowel ɯ, and
#     the apostrophe after a consonant (गोजौ कमा: खर’, दख’ना) writes its
#     inherent vowel;
#   the first article of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights in Bodo
#     Devanagari and in the Latin orthography (Wikipedia): ै is ɯi, ं is ŋ,
#     a bare consonant before another consonant or at the end of a word has
#     no vowel.
# The inherent vowel is ɔ; ै ौ are ɯi ɯu; य and व after a vowel and before a
# consonant or the end of the word are the second half of a diphthong
# (थाखाय tʰakʰai, दाव dau). The Indo-Aryan letters of loanwords are read with
# Bodo sounds: घ झ ढ ध भ as ɡ z d d b, च छ श ष as s, ज as z, ट ठ ड ण as t tʰ d n,
# ड़ ढ़ as r. Vowel length is not distinctive (ई ऊ = इ उ). Tone is not
# written in Bodo Devanagari, and this reading does not say it. The apostrophe
# reaches this file as ॉ (lib.rs) or, inside number words, as itself.

[meta]
tag = brx
block = 0900
stress = initial
final-secondary = no
deletion = medial
inherent = ɔ ɔ
anusvara = velar
visarga = h
geminate = k ɡ t d p b m n ŋ l s z r j w
geminate-aspirate = k t p

